+# Repository Guidelines
+
+## Project Structure & Module Organization
+
+This repository is a Telink platform SDK organized by hardware, shared middleware, demos, and build tooling.
+
+- `chip/`: MCU-specific startup, drivers, link scripts, and low-level libraries, grouped by chip family such as `B92`, `B91`, `TL321X`, and `W92`.
+- `common/`: shared SDK utilities, USB debug support, and common protocol code.
+- `demo/vendor/`: application demos. The active USB work is under `demo/vendor/USB_Demo/`.
+- `demo/vendor/common/`: shared demo configuration and board support.
+- `cmake_configs/`: generated/maintained CMake target descriptions, for example `TL_PLATFORM_SDK_B92_cmake.json`.
+- `tools/`: SDK helper scripts for config generation, firmware checks, secure boot, and release packaging.
+- `cmake_builds/` and `compile_commands.json`: generated build outputs; avoid hand-editing them.
+
+## Build, Test, and Development Commands
+
+Configure the B92 CMake build:
+
+```bash
+cmake -S . -B cmake_builds/TL_PLATFORM_SDK_B92/TL32_ELF_MCULIB_V5F_GCC12_2 -G Ninja \
+  -DPROJECT_NAME=TL_PLATFORM_SDK_B92 \
+  -DTOOLCHAIN_NAME="TL32 ELF MCULIB V5F GCC12.2" \
+  -DTOOLCHAIN_PATH=/home/soyo/Telink/toolchain/RDS/V5.3.x/toolchains/nds32le-elf-mculib-v5f
+```
+
+Build the current USB demo:
+
+```bash
+cmake --build cmake_builds/TL_PLATFORM_SDK_B92/TL32_ELF_MCULIB_V5F_GCC12_2 --target USB_Demo
+```
+
+The build runs SDK pre/post scripts and produces `.elf`, `.bin`, and `.lst` artifacts in the build directory.
+
+## Coding Style & Naming Conventions
+
+The codebase is C-oriented and compiled with strict warnings (`-Werror`, `-Wall`, `-Wextra`). Follow nearby file style: 4-space indentation, lower_snake_case function names, uppercase macros, and SDK attributes such as `_attribute_ram_code_sec_` where timing requires RAM execution. Keep comments short and only where they clarify hardware timing or protocol behavior.
+
+## Testing Guidelines
+
+There is no centralized unit-test framework in this tree. Validate changes by building the affected target and, for firmware behavior, testing on hardware with the relevant PC-side tool or logic analyzer. For USB CDC changes, verify enumeration, DTR/open-port behavior, endpoint timing, and protocol byte-stream compatibility.
+
+## Commit & Pull Request Guidelines
+
+Recent commits use scoped conventional-style subjects, for example `feat(tl321x)(sys): add ret_ldo voltage calibration`. Prefer concise subjects with chip/module scopes: `fix(b92)(usb): stabilize cdc tx queue`. Pull requests should describe the target chip/demo, build command used, hardware validation performed, and any protocol or timing impact.
+
+## Agent-Specific Instructions
+
+Do not overwrite generated build artifacts unless a build command updates them. Keep edits scoped to the active target and preserve unrelated local changes.
